A||||3||WisconsinGT||Dogbone question||||1||
Z||000000||WisconsinGT||09-03-2000||12:52 AM||||I have the fierostore poly Dogbone. I want to replace it tomorrow. I looked at the instructions at the Fiero online service guide web site. Someone wrote that moving the car will allow the dogbone to line up for an east hook-up. Sounds good. But is there anything I have to be concerned about when removing the old stock dogbone? i.e. will the engine move? I am assuming that there is alot of pressure on the bolts. Will this create a problem trying to remove the bolts? Thanks folks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||09-03-2000||01:02 AM||||Not to perspire.  There usually isn't a lot of preload on the dogbone - and if there is, the load on the attach bolt will keep you from sliding the bolt out easily.<P>So if the bolt won't come out, just move the car (assuming yours has a manual tranny, and you leave it in gear) to line up the holes as you slide the bolt out.  Same thing when you reinstall the bolt.  Good luck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> !  <P> ||||reg||6
Z||000002||theogre||09-03-2000||11:33 AM||||If you are getting much load in the strut check the motor mounts. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||09-03-2000||01:37 PM||||Definitely what ogre said.  The last owner/loser of the Grape Ape let the dogbone bushings go for so long that the motor mounts (2 of them) broke.  It was fairly obvious since, even with the dogbone still in place (basically a piece of metal with a few shreds of rubber attached by that point), the motor had shifted a couple of inches.||||reg||1
